WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:10.710
I'd really like to welcome our
first keynote speakers, Mine

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.010
Çetinkaya-Rundel and Julia
Lowndes, who are going

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.460
to be talking about Quarto.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.530
And there there are literally
like no two better people

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.170
suited to give this talk.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.590
Mine is my
colleague at RStudio.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.340
She's also my co-author
of R for Data Science.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.250
She is the professor
of the practice at Duke

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.260
and just a fantastic educator.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.320
And I deeply appreciate
her empathy with new R users.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.250
Also, whenever a correctly type
Mine's full name, including

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.370
the little dangly cedilla
on the ç,

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.040
I give myself a self high five.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.380
Julia Lowndes is a PhD
in marine biology and squids.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:03.550
She also started OpenScapes
in 2018, supported by Mozilla.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.050
Julia is like
one of the people

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.900
I just love to hang
out with at RStudio conf

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.600
and whenever I see her
because she is just

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.850
so passionate about educating
people about the power of R

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.250
and whenever I get
to hang out with her,

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.890
I just feel rejuvenated
and excited about the work

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:22.970
that I do.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.010
Unfortunately, Julia
and Mine will not

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.780
be taking answering
questions today,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.810
but if you do ask questions on
the Slido, they will read them

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.900
and they will pass
them on to the Quarto team.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.820
But they will not be
taking questions today.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.505
So please join me in
welcoming Julia and Mine.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.330
Thanks so much, Hadley, and
Thanks so much to all of you.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.170
We are so excited to be here.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.360
Mine and I have been using
Quarto for over a year now.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.500
Mine has been teaching with
it and has been contributing

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.860
to the development of it,
and my teams with Openscapes

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.830
were the first external
testers of Quarto.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.260
And as we've used it
over the past year,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.780
we have grown to love
it so much and we

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.450
are excited to
welcome you to Quarto.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.460
But also we know
you have questions.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.710
We did too.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.250
What is Quarto?

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.750
Why is Quarto? And how
does it interoperate

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.210
with R Markdown and your
other existing workflows?

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:36.970
Well, Quarto unifies and
extends the R Markdown ecosystem.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.400
It unifies it for
people who love

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.520
R Markdown so that
little points of friction

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.530
are reduced as you make
websites and books and slides.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.210
All of those will now
have a common approach

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.570
and it extends it for
people who do not yet know

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.060
R Markdown
as a friendly way

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.300
to work reproducibly
and publish documents

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:03.780
that combine text and code
and output in the same place.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.350
So Quarto is a new open-source,
scientific

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.390
and technical publishing system.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.060
And the goal is to
make the process

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.870
of creating and collaborating
dramatically better.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.970
So it handles inputs in
multiple languages and outputs

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.930
in multiple formats, all
going through the same Quarto

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.920
machinery.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.340
So in our talk today, we'll
introduce you to Quarto

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.290
through stories and demos.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:40.260
First, Mine will focus on how
Quarto unifies the R Markdown

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.360
ecosystem through stories
of sharing and teaching.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.040
And then I'll focus on how Quarto
extends the R Markdown ecosystem

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.650
through collaborating
and reimagining.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.180
So we are so excited
to get started.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.840
So let's kick it off
with you Mine to talk

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.300
about sharing with Quarto.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.210
Thanks Julia.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.330
I'm so excited to
have the opportunity

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.010
to talk to you
about Quarto, which

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.070
I've been living and
breathing over the last year.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.670
So I work at the intersection
of education, research

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:12.940
and development.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.150
The word educator
might make you think

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.450
that I am in front of crowds
like this, maybe a bit smaller,

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.950
24/7 lecturing at them.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.380
But actually I spend a lot of
my time in front of my computer

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.600
creating and authoring
artifacts that I

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.540
share with the world for
the purpose of teaching.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.310
And everything
I create, I share openly

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:41.970
with an open license that allows
for use, reuse and adaptation.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.000
I often think web-first
for wider reach,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.160
but I also still
live in the confines

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.370
of academia and traditional
publishing, which

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.360
means that I still have
to make a lot of PDFs.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.340
Therefore, an
authoring system that

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:02.280
allows me to achieve multiple
outputs from the same inputs,

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.050
the same source code that
brings code and prose together

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:12.990
is not only essential for me,
but it's actually, basically

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.610
what I need to carry
on my existence.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.600
And for the last decade
that has been

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.100
R Markdown for me.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.270
In fact, I remember very
vividly when JJ and Yihui

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.280
gave a talk at UseR 2012.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.350
The first UseR I
attended in Nashville.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.970
So I'll kind of go
back to that time when

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.620
I attended that
conference, I don't

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.805
think I would have identified
myself as an R person.

00:05:40.805 --> 00:05:42.930
In fact, I'm pretty sure
we went to that conference

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.790
because we were going
to Bonnaroo already

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.320
and it was there and
I thought, why not?

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:51.960
This was the summer of my
first year of teaching at Duke.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.180
I was very dedicated
to open science.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.500
I had already been working
on an open source textbook,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.570
but this idea of wider
reach and open science,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.880
but also doing computing,
I had not really

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.450
put the pieces together.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.230
And one of the reasons why I had
not put those pieces together

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.090
is that I didn't
have a good workflow

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.860
to bring them together.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:19.260
If you know me, you know that I'm
a prolific sharer and I

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.940
like things to look pretty as
well, not just be functional.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:23.440
And pretty.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.230
And I really did not
have a good workflow

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.130
for being able to achieve that.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.180
But I started listening
to this keynote on knitr,

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.030
and it must have been no
more than 3 slides into it

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.740
that I just actually
pulled out my laptop

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.660
and started converting my
teaching materials to use it.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.830
Because this idea that I
can bring narrative and then

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:44.490
code together and have it just
be like syntax highlighted

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:47.820
and look organized and be ready
to be published on the web

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.120
was just such a dream
come true for me.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.520
And by the end of
that keynote, I

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.490
was already brainstorming
ways of not just me

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.310
using it, but thinking
about how can I teach this

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.050
to my intro students?

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.690
So I am going to call
this keynote a success.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:08.290
if some of you have
already tuned me out,

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:11.550
taken out your laptops
and have gone to quarto.org

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.640
to start playing with it.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.380
But I do hope that you will
continue to listen with one ear

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.370
and taking these beautiful
illustrations by Alison Horst.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.860
And for those of
you who want to have

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.380
a more laid back morning,
you can go to this link:

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.780
mine.quarto.pub/

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.060
hello-quarto

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.650
Sit back and I will
advance the slides for you.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:43.380
So Julia mentioned that
about unifying the R Markdown

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:44.290
ecosystem.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.680
So let's dig into that
a little bit more.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:50.010
Here are some of
the packages

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.260
from the R Markdown
ecosystem that you've

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:56.080
come to love and rely
on over the years.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.770
Each package addresses
a different need

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.650
and solves a different problem.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.960
You might be creating
websites or writing blogs

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.780
with blogdown or distill
writing books with bookdown,

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.090
writing journal
articles with articles

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:14.550
package. Quarto unifies
the functionality

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:17.610
of these packages on
the technical side,

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.700
building on pandoc
and on the human side

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.900
building on over a
decade of experience

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.360
developing, maintaining
and tweaking

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.000
these packages as
well as community

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.169
feedback and the community, and

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:36.870
the extension package is
developed by the community.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:40.500
And such a unification
process presents a really nice

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.590
opportunity to
identify and address

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.860
gaps in functionality
and inefficiencies

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.340
in user experience.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:51.540
And developing Quarto
has been no different.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.120
So some highlights
of these improvements

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.040
that are near and
dear to my heart

00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:00.240
are a consistent implementation
of attractive and handy

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.320
features across outputs: tabsets,
code-folding, syntax

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:05.230
highlighting.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.320
Imagine these things
just working out

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.620
of the box regardless of
what you're producing.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.170
Also more accessible
defaults as well

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.520
as better support for
creating accessible documents

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.450
guardrails that are particularly
helpful for new learners,

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:25.800
things like YAML completion
and informative syntax errors.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:28.710
And perhaps excitingly,
for those of you

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.890
who are not coming to this
from the R Markdown ecosystem,

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.220
support for other
languages like Python, Julia

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.830
observable via
the Jupyter engine

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.940
for executable code chunks.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:43.370
So by now I am
assuming you've already

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.440
started playing with
Quarto or you're just

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:46.950
waiting for me to do that.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:51.260
So let's dive in and take a look
at what all the Quarto fuss is about.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:55.430
So here I have something that
might look familiar: an 

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:57.080
R Markdown document.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.790
Let's try this one more time.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:05.160
An R Markdown document
that has some YAML on top,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.040
some text and some code.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.020
Many of you have
seen this before.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.350
Let's knit this document.

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:13.830
It's a very, very
simple analysis

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.590
using the 
palmerpenguins data set

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.740
that has a nice
visualization at the bottom.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.010
So what I'm going to
do first is switch over

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.660
to the visual editor.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.130
I personally really like
writing in the visual editor.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:31.500
It allows you to do things
like can bold things

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:35.200
and italicize things without
having to write Markdown code.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.960
And Julia will do a demo of the
visual editor a bit more later.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.120
But I'd like to show
that whether you're

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.880
using the visual or the source
editor with R Markdown,

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.660
the output looks
exactly the same.

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.720
So it's just about your
personal preference

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.660
about authoring
on the input side.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.460
But what we're going to do next
is actually change things up.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:55.750
Let's close that file.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.630
And what I'm going
to do is just rename

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:02.310
that RMD to be a QMD
not touch anything else.

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.720
The little logo
changes, I open it up.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.670
And what I have is a render
button instead of a knit button

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.710
and I can see that the
document just works.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:14.790
So Quarto is able to render
R Markdown documents.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.520
The output looks a little bit
different, slightly different

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.820
styling, but we haven't
changed anything else.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.760
We're going to
change the YAML now

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.240
to include the
format field instead

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:30.280
of output, which is how we
define different format types.

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:31.860
I'm going to click
on render and save

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.500
so that I don't
have to click Render

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.340
every time I make changes.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:40.410
So again, this is basically
our starting place with Quarto.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.730
I can do different output
formats, so let's create a PDF.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:49.050
One of the things I really like
about creating PDFs with Quarto

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.660
as a person who doesn't
like creating PDFs

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.450
is that the PDF appears
in the viewer as opposed

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:55.920
to in a pop up window.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:59.970
But let's go back to
HTML and take a look

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:01.990
at some of these chunk options.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:04.230
So with Quarto, we're
using the hash pipe

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.450
for defining our
chunk options, which

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.910
I think helps with
readability a lot

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.670
and we have really nice
completion for YAML.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.160
So if I can't
figure out what was

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:18.330
that chunk option for
hiding my chunks oh echo,

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:23.380
how would I define my the
values, true or false?

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.975
I can rely on the YAML
completion to do that.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:32.300
And now on the output, we
don't have that code chunk

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.890
appearing anymore.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:38.750
I can also set global chunks.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:42.230
And maybe like me, this is one
of your most Googled things.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:44.500
How do I set global
chunks and knitr?

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.470
I can actually just do
that now in the YAML

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:50.710
through the execute
field so I can turn off

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:55.000
the display of code chunks
for the entire document very

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.680
explicitly at the top.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.530
And now they're all gone.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:07.680
Well, we've been working with
an HTML document which is going

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:09.730
to produce web ready output.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:11.850
Let's take a look
at one more thing.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:15.150
One of the reasons why I
like these chunk options

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:19.140
as hash pipes is when you have
really long chunk options, say,

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:23.970
if I want to insert this lengthy
text for as alternative text

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.910
for the plot that
I am producing,

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:29.560
it stays very kind of legible.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:30.990
And obviously the
alternative text

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:33.220
is not going to be
visible in the output.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:34.920
But if I was to
inspect that element,

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.500
I can see that text is
in the HTML source code.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:41.120
So that is still accessible
to screen readers.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.290
Making these things
easier is going

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:46.510
to make more folks
write alternative text.

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:50.400
I think for the visualizations
that they're producing,

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.010
let's make a presentation.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.330
I don't need to touch
the rest of my code.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:59.610
I am just going to change
my YAML header to format,

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:03.810
revealjs and create a
revealjs presentation.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:06.600
My title page is
basically whatever

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.340
was in the YAML of my document.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:12.570
And you can see that each
of the section headers

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:13.840
are now new slides.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.680
I have that really squished
plot because the revealjs tries

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:20.400
to help me by making my
plots take just as much space

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:24.960
as there is, but I can
easily insert another header,

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:27.090
a second level
header to just move

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:29.075
that content to the next slide.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.590
And to me, being able to
do this without touching

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:40.640
the rest of the content is
really, really satisfying.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:47.100
All right.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.830
So let's go back to HTML.

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.980
And next, what
we're going to do is

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.930
attempt to make a website
in less than 10 minutes.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:58.500
So I have a single
HTML document here.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:00.300
I'm going to create
one more document.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.800
So we're basically expanding
from a single document

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:07.250
to bringing multiple
documents together

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:10.860
in a Quarto project.

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.130
I'm going to keep this
document very simple

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:15.480
intentionally no
computing in there.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.760
We're just going to put
a little bit of text.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.250
So I've titled my
document welcome.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.940
And maybe let's add some
plain text like "This

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:26.550
is the demo site for the talk".

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.330
Next, let's bring them together.

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:32.290
I'm going to create a YAML
file, a _quarto.yml file.

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.930
And I am going to
write YAML on the fly.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:38.650
And I can do that relying
on the completion again.

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:41.710
So we're creating
a Quarto project.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:44.460
There are various types
of Quarto projects

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:45.870
you can create.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:49.730
The type where I'm going
to select here is website.

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:51.740
And then I am
going to define how

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:53.700
I want to lay out my website.

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:54.850
Let's give it a title.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:58.940
I'll call it Quarto and I'm going
to create a navigation bar.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.490
And on the left side
of the navigation bar,

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:04.850
I'm going to link to the
two documents I have,

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:08.900
the index.qmd I just
created and the 

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:13.730
Hello.qmd that I had renamed from
that original R Markdown file.

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.590
The next thing
I'm going to do is,

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:18.710
I like to stay in
the RStudio IDE, but I

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:22.380
want my build tab to be able
to get my website going.

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:26.300
So I'm just going to restart
my project, my RStudio project,

00:16:26.300 --> 00:16:28.940
and I have a build tab
that gives me the option

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:32.065
to render or preview a website.

00:16:32.065 --> 00:16:34.315
Let's render the website and
see what that looks like.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:42.470
And there we go.

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:45.450
We have a website with
multiple pages in it

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.690
that we have very
clearly written out

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:51.880
in this simple and
expressive YAML file.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.326
To me, this is
incredibly encouraging. [applause]

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:06.420
Next what I'm going to do is I'm
going to start setting things

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:07.270
up for Julia.

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.730
So and also try to
be kind to myself.

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:12.910
I have lots of computation
and that hello.qmd

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:15.750
Let's assume and I don't want
this to constantly keep running

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.290
every time I update things.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:19.349
So I'm going to set
the freeze feature

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:22.980
to true, which says only
rerun the computation when

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:26.079
I explicitly ask you to
do so in that document.

00:17:26.079 --> 00:17:27.900
Otherwise, as I
change other things,

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:29.770
don't rerun the computation.

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:31.320
You can see that
when I set this,

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:35.490
I get this freeze folder where
the results of my computation

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:38.310
is stored and I can
set freeze to be

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:41.910
other values like automatic,
where it would only update

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:44.340
if you actually
touch that file, I'm

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.490
going to set it equal to
true so that let's see

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:51.810
if Julia can later recreate this
website in a non-R environment.

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:55.080
So I have a website, I have
stored my computations,

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:56.980
I have multiple pages.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:57.710
What next?

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:09.625
Yeah we should be
able to publish it.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:15.320
All right.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:19.720
Well, we could with
one command! 

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:23.290
quarto publish. I write quarto publish
in the terminal to

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:25.020
publish this to quarto.pub.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:27.850
So if you do want to see
the published website,

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:29.950
I'm not sure why the
video is hanging,

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:32.170
but if you do want to see
the published website,

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:39.370
you could go to mine.quarto.pub/hello-demo

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.940
and the website is there, the
two pages that we created,

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:45.970
and we're able to do
that without setting up

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:48.940
things like actions to run
our code and whatnot with one

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:51.800
line from the terminal.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:52.410
All right.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:55.280
So what did we see here?

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:58.760
Now that you've all had a chance
to kind of see Quarto in action,

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:02.827
you might be wondering, how are
R Markdown in Quarto different?

00:19:02.827 --> 00:19:04.910
Especially because we were
able to just get things

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:06.950
going by renaming that file.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:09.590
The main difference is
that with R Markdown,

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:12.380
it is the R package,
R Markdown that

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:14.390
does the heavy
lifting of what needs

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:18.540
to happen to go from the
RMD file to your output.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:22.180
And that's something we as R
users are very accustomed to.

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:25.130
You want to solve a problem,
install that packages

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:27.440
the package that
solves the problem

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.550
you want to solve. With Quarto

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:32.550
on the other hand, it's
the command-line interface,

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:33.630
so not an R package

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.255
but a command-line
interface, something

00:19:35.255 --> 00:19:37.490
like Git for example,
that's actually

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:41.660
doing the heavy lifting
of taking your input file,

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:45.470
running your executable code
chunks through either knitr

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:48.980
or jupyter, creating
a plaintext Markdown

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:52.460
file that then
uses pandoc to get

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:55.610
to the output of your choice.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:59.240
So this is technically
pretty impressive,

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:01.790
but I'm going to be
very honest with you,

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:05.450
it's not exactly what sparked
my interest about Quarto.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:07.730
What did spark my
interest about Quarto

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:13.020
was how easy it makes it to
move between various formats.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:16.820
So, as an educator, the things
that are of utmost importance

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:20.930
to me is that the artifacts
I create are reproducible

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:23.060
and that there is an
ease of transition

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:25.390
between various output formats.

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:28.160
At the risk of sounding
like a broken record,

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:31.910
I do actually have to
relay the same information

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:37.640
to my students in slides as kind
of lecture notes in a homework

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:40.260
assignment posted on
the course website.

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:43.100
So oftentimes I'm
moving the same content

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:45.350
around these different formats.

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:48.230
And over the last year
of doing this with Quarto,

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:51.170
I felt like I finally
found the tool that

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:55.190
allows me to do this easily
without futzing around

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:59.730
with my code and the
content part of my document.

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:02.630
But just changing a couple
of things in the YAML.

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:07.190
For example, if I want to
lesson kind of a single document

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:11.120
as lecture notes or something,
I can say format HTML,

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:14.390
I can easily change that
to revealjs without touching

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:17.330
anything else in my document
and I have a slide deck,

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:24.650
I can simply call that file in
a _quarto.yml file to create a website.

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:28.970
And you can see that the
all I needed to change

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:31.250
is just the YAML.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:33.420
I didn't have to
remove slide breaks.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.930
I didn't have to
re-level headers.

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:37.500
That does save me some time.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:40.940
And that time can go to
actually focus on the content

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:46.060
that I'm developing and that
I find extremely liberating.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:49.990
But ultimately for
the kind of easily,

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:54.490
while easily creating
documents in multiple formats,

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:57.420
is nice for the sharing
story to be complete.

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:00.920
The tool needs to support
easily sharing things as well.

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:04.030
So something like
that, quarto publish command

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:06.610
that you can run in the
terminal and I'm sure soon

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:08.800
a button in the RStudio IDE.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:10.750
I think that tooling
that makes it

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:14.440
easy to share what you do
with the world on the web

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:16.330
is incredibly useful.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:19.300
And it's not just useful
to you, but to the world

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:22.210
as it facilitates open
sharing of knowledge

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:25.570
for learning and reuse.

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.700
I've been talking
about me and my work

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:31.420
so far, but I, like
many of you, don't

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:34.960
operate in a silo
disconnected from others.

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:38.760
Much of my work and much of
Julia's work is collaborative.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:42.340
So over to you, Julia, for
collaborating with Quarto.

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:44.950
Thanks, Mine.

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:48.010
I have gained so much from
the open sharing and the web

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:50.140
first approach from
the R community,

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:53.200
and it has so informed
the way that I collaborate

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:55.920
and the way that I work.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:59.280
So I work at the intersection
of actionable environmental

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:02.560
science, open science
and data science.

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:05.970
And what I absolutely love
doing is mentoring, teaching

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:07.630
and empowering communities.

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:10.290
Having learned from so
many of you, like I said,

00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:14.280
and I wanted to pay this
forward and welcome more folks

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.640
to this experience that I've
had with the R and Mozilla

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:19.290
communities in particular.

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:23.460
So I've made this my
job through Openscapes.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:26.070
Openscapes is an
approach and a movement

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:28.890
that I launched in 2018
and have built together

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:31.200
with my partner, Erin Robinson.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:34.170
Our flagship activity is
mentoring research teams

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:35.540
and open science.

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:38.250
We think of open science
as a beautiful landscape

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.580
full of open source software,
data and communities

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:44.610
and many paths forward.

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:48.210
And we help people move
from the sad, lonely animals

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.250
in the foreground,
on the bottom left

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:52.890
and towards this
landscape, so that they

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:56.400
can explore this and navigate
this safely with their teams.

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:00.180
And our role is to welcome
folks at the trailhead

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:03.460
and help them establish what
that trailhead even looks like.

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:05.620
Who's there, who is
part of the team?

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:08.790
How can we think about who
can be included and start

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:11.430
this journey together
with limited trampling

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.920
and frustration?

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:16.830
So we work with professional
research groups in government,

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:20.040
academia and nonprofits,
and we help these teams

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:23.490
modernize their workflows
and shift their culture

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:26.010
with their teams and
within their organizations

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:29.760
through open source software
and open source practices.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:32.460
Adyan Rios from
NOAA Fisheries

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:37.020
has been part of Openscapes,
and she describes in this way.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:39.000
She says, as
fisheries scientists,

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.340
we know that when
we're in rough seas,

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:45.250
it's important to keep the
fish afloat.

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:47.610
It's also important to
keep the ship afloat

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:50.890
and to get out of
the storm, she says.

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:52.890
Openscapes helps
teams steer out

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:56.130
of the storm of email
chains with 37 versions

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:58.620
of the same spreadsheet
and into the calmer

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:02.310
waters of open science and
meaningful collaboration using

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:04.410
tools like R.

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.970
So I think that I
have the best job.

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:09.450
I get to mentor and
collaborate with teams

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:13.350
like Adyan's who are doing
really critical and hard work

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.440
around environment and climate.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:19.680
And I get to help them do it
a little better by connecting

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.890
them to the open source
tools and community resources

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:25.170
that we all produce.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:28.320
And it really blends working
with these teams really blends

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:31.500
for me the role of mentoring
and collaborating because I

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:34.270
learned so much along the way.

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:37.250
And this is definitely true
with our story with NASA.

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:46.190
With NASA, you probably
think about outer space,

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:50.340
but they collect an unbelievable
amount of data about Earth.

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:53.840
And researchers used
Earth data in many ways,

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:56.360
including how to understand
impacts of the heat

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:58.670
waves and the wildfires
that we're increasingly

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:01.730
seeing through the summers
due to climate change.

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:04.490
These data are freely available
from 12 different data

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:07.550
centers across NASA,
but researchers

00:26:07.550 --> 00:26:09.380
have to download
these data locally

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:12.510
from each of these data
centers to analyze it locally.

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:15.140
And this puts a huge
burden on the researchers.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:18.380
So now, following many
years of preparation,

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:22.800
these data are migrating to the
cloud through AWS and others.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.120
And NASA's data centers will
be supporting researchers

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:28.910
who will be migrating
their analytical workflows

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:31.430
to the cloud too.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:34.580
So my partner Erin had 10
years of experience working

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:37.940
with the NASA Earth data community,
and she brought NASA and open

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:42.260
scapes together in a three year
project that we lead together.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:45.500
And our focus is on
developing, first and foremost,

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:49.500
a mentor community across
those 12 data centers.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:51.890
So that they can
co-create and teach

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:55.010
common tutorials for
these researchers

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:58.040
using NASA's Earth data.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:01.730
So let me just stop here and
say that this is a big project

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:05.810
and it is awesome, but it was
also incredibly intimidating

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:06.890
to me.

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:09.210
I do not know the
NASA community.

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:12.890
This is an organization I've
looked up to my whole life.

00:27:12.890 --> 00:27:16.130
I'm not familiar with these
types of huge spatial data

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:17.540
that they're working with.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:19.760
The type of research
questions or scientists

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:23.300
are asking and modeling, not to
mention cloud infrastructure,

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:26.300
which is a whole new era.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.850
But an additional thing that
was particularly intimidating

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:32.450
to me as an R person is
that many Earth scientists

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:37.130
use Python and they are
developing tutorials primarily

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:38.590
in Jupyter Notebooks.

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:41.300
And my role in leading
NASA's Openscapes

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:43.820
was not to learn Python
from the ground up,

00:27:43.820 --> 00:27:46.700
but I needed to be able to
collaborate and interoperate

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.220
and support Python users.

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:51.620
So something really
powerful, though,

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:54.980
is that I brought almost
a decade of experience

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:57.080
and confidence
using R Markdown,

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:00.288
and I've been able
to, you've probably

00:28:00.288 --> 00:28:02.580
heard me talk about this
because I talk about it a lot,

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:05.870
I feel like it's a superpower.

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:10.160
And Quarto let me bring
this superpower to Python.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:12.590
Quarto is emerging right
when we needed it,

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:17.300
and I asked JJ if we could use
Quarto for NASA's Openscapes.

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:19.250
It was really early
in its development

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:22.700
and this became a really
exciting collaboration

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:26.080
between the Quarto developer
team and the NASA mentors.

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:30.140
This was a really generous
and gracious group of people

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:33.740
together as we were
learning how to collaborate

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:37.010
together and build tutorials
and try new software,

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:40.310
and we were able to, through
our questions and use influence

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:44.690
some of the design features,
particularly the freeze feature

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:48.770
and how it operates
with Jupyter Notebooks.

00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:51.380
So this is the
first Quarto site we

00:28:51.380 --> 00:28:54.500
developed for our first
teaching event last fall.

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:58.070
This is where we really found
traction as a mentor community,

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:00.680
and we were able to make
this beautiful collection

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:02.540
of tutorials that
was, first of all,

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:04.752
helpful for our own
development as we were learning

00:29:04.752 --> 00:29:06.710
to collaborate with each
other because we could

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:09.200
see which tutorials
were there already

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:10.720
and where the gaps were.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:14.210
But it was also a very
friendly, contained place

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:18.780
for our learners to navigate
through these resources.

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:21.210
And importantly, Quarto
was also something

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:24.188
that we were all able
to learn together,

00:29:24.188 --> 00:29:26.730
and that turned out to be really
important, because it turned

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:29.730
out I wasn't the only person
who was intimidated by all

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:32.580
these moving pieces on all
these new pieces of software

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:34.270
and how to collaborate together.

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:36.750
Learning Quarto together
opened the door for us

00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:40.590
to talk more about our workflows
and develop better systems

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:43.920
for collaborating and really
take this to the next level.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:45.990
And from all of our
investment and workflows,

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:48.450
we were then able
to rapidly create

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:51.120
additional resources for
the other teaching workshops

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:52.830
that we were leading
that were designed

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:55.800
to meet those learners where
they were and interoperate

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:58.020
between the materials
we developed.

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:00.600
So there's two things
to highlight here.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:02.820
The first is that Quarto
enables collaborating

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:04.670
across coding languages.

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:07.470
This is one peak of a
way to think about this.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:10.020
This is a simplified view
of one of our YAML files

00:30:10.020 --> 00:30:13.230
where you can see we've got
multiple file types here.

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:16.620
These are mostly we've mostly
focused on ipynb Jupyter

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.650
Notebook files in our
primary development so far.

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:22.680
But as we've worked more and
more with the NASA research

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:25.650
community, we've discovered
that's not the right word.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:30.390
We've found that there is a
substantial R community using

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:33.060
NASA Earth data cloud, and
it's really exciting

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:34.800
that the workflow
we've invested in

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:37.170
and have developed
together we can use

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:39.920
to support R users as well.

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:42.830
The second is that Quarto
enables contributing

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:44.510
from our current tools.

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:48.230
So some of our team,
mainly me and Erin,

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:52.340
used RStudio primarily
some used jupyter lab VS

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:55.100
code, some directly,
some edit directly

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:58.283
in GitHub through the editor.

00:30:58.283 --> 00:31:00.200
And this was really
important that we were all

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:03.200
able to use our own tools
because with all these moving

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:06.170
pieces, we were
able to integrate

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:09.290
that workflow into the
comfort of our own tools.

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:11.030
So let me show you
what Quarto looks

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:12.986
like in Jupyter Notebooks.

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:21.160
So this is a Jupyter
Notebook and it's actually

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:23.015
running in our Jupyter Hub.

00:31:23.015 --> 00:31:24.640
You can see we're
going to interoperate

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:27.040
with Quarto in the
terminal here on the right,

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:30.670
and we've got all the files that
Mine just created on the left.

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:33.420
So the first thing we'll do
is we'll say Quarto preview.

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:40.950
And this will open this
will provide a URL where

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:44.040
we can review the previewed
version of the site

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:45.610
that Mine has created.

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:48.450
So I don't have
R installed here,

00:31:48.450 --> 00:31:52.470
but with the freeze
function I'm able to open

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:55.170
in this new environment.

00:31:55.170 --> 00:31:56.980
So here is our
beautiful website.

00:31:56.980 --> 00:31:59.130
I like to kind of
have them side by side

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:00.310
so I can see them refresh.

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:03.210
And if we want to add a Jupyter
Notebook, let's say, oh, look,

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:04.180
I just added one.

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:05.920
Great let's have a look at it.

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:14.530
So this is a Jupyter Notebook
I've got over here on this tab.

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:17.780
And this is how we
can think about Quarto.

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:21.130
We've got our YAML at the top
where we're going to say Hello

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:23.540
to Quarto penguins in Python.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:26.200
We've got R Markdown
text here and we're

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:29.030
going to use the palmerpenguins
package in Python.

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:32.950
This is how we'll load
the packages and the way

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:34.990
you operate this in
a Jupyter Notebook

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:37.580
is that you execute
the code as you go.

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:39.370
So when I press
this play button,

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:42.610
this is going to
execute that code

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:45.640
and actually embedded
in the document.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:49.780
So we'll do that again
with a plot about penguins

00:32:49.780 --> 00:32:52.310
by clicking this button.

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:56.920
And now this plot is here
as part of the document.

00:32:56.920 --> 00:33:00.460
So the workflow here is that
you execute the code as you go

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:03.650
and as you develop and then
you save your file as you go.

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:08.070
So this is the workflow
in a Jupyter Notebook.

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:10.240
And when we save that.

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:12.060
Now we can think
about adding this

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:14.460
to our _quarto.yml
and the way we do

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:17.290
that is exactly the
way that Mine did.

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:20.340
Before I can add a Jupyter
Notebook right here next

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:21.440
to these Quarto files.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:31.610
And when we save
this YAML file, we're

00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:33.990
able to peek at the terminal.

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:36.570
That's going to tell us
when the site refreshes.

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:41.860
And there is our Indeed
our Hello Quarto in Python.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:43.310
You can see it's beautiful.

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:46.950
And it looks exactly the same
as the QMD documents in R.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:51.640
So let's play around
a little bit more.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:57.230
And I'll show you two
more things in Quarto.

00:33:57.230 --> 00:34:01.550
So if we wanted to add a
figure caption to this plot,

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:06.650
we would use the same
hash pipe comment system

00:34:06.650 --> 00:34:09.750
that Mine just introduced.

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:11.820
So I can put this
figure caption here

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:14.170
and again, I can
execute and save.

00:34:14.170 --> 00:34:19.300
And this when I
refresh over here.

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:23.710
This will show up
in our document

00:34:23.710 --> 00:34:29.409
and there is our header or
yes, our figure caption.

00:34:29.409 --> 00:34:30.962
But you can see
that it's not here.

00:34:30.962 --> 00:34:32.920
It doesn't do anything
to the Jupyter Notebook.

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:35.510
It's a comment in the
Jupiter ecosystem.

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:39.260
This it's not affecting the
Jupyter Notebook at all.

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:42.929
Let's add one other feature
here in the comment.

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:43.719
I'm sorry.

00:34:43.719 --> 00:34:45.380
In the code chunk option.

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:54.840
Let's say we wanted to
display this plot in the margin

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:55.449
instead.

00:34:55.449 --> 00:35:01.170
This is a style that
that's quite popular.

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:04.140
So I might have to make this
a little bit wider so we

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:04.940
can have a look.

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.610
A little bit wider.

00:35:11.610 --> 00:35:15.465
There is our plot in the
margin from a Jupyter Notebook.

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:20.200
Woo! [applause]

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:32.790
so let's now say that
operating between formats

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:34.170
is really important,
particularly

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:35.500
when we're in collaborating.

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:38.280
So a workflow that's
been really powerful

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:42.690
is to convert Jupyter
Notebooks into Quarto documents

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:45.960
so that checking
them in with GitHub

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:48.160
can reduce merge conflicts.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:52.770
So what I can do here is to
say Quarto convert and tell it

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:57.210
the file and it will by
default turn it into a QMD

00:35:57.210 --> 00:35:59.980
and you can go back and
forth between these formats.

00:35:59.980 --> 00:36:03.460
So you can see it's created
a new QMD document.

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:05.340
And when I open it
in the editor here,

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:09.570
this is now that same document
with our familiar view

00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:12.485
in QMD form, and you can
see the Python code there.

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:20.290
So this has a lot of
utility and power,

00:36:20.290 --> 00:36:22.770
and it's very important
for teaching as well.

00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:26.010
So Mine, I'll turn it over
to you for teaching.

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:26.800
Thank you.

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:29.700
So over the last decade,
I have thoroughly

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:32.670
enjoyed teaching
with R Markdown.

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:35.250
When you get students in
the class for the first time

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:38.700
and you give them like a fully
functional R Markdown document

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:41.010
and ask them to hit
knit and they can

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:44.340
see their text and their
code and the output

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:47.525
come together before they
even learn any programming.

00:36:47.525 --> 00:36:48.900
You can almost
hear a 'wow, that's

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:50.620
so cool' in the classroom.

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:53.550
And I've also loved that
this tool is something

00:36:53.550 --> 00:36:55.950
you can introduce to
introductory students

00:36:55.950 --> 00:37:00.450
on the first day of class and
that it can grow with them.

00:37:00.450 --> 00:37:06.270
However, as students expertise
grow in statistics and also

00:37:06.270 --> 00:37:09.810
in computing and
also their needs

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.940
grow in terms of the
different outputs they

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:15.000
need to produce to say,
just a single homework

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:17.610
document that they
can turn in as an HTML

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:21.690
to a well formatted journal
article or a thesis that

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:23.940
meets the school style.

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:26.580
There is a little bit
of friction that happens

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:30.780
and one a pleasant
surprise for me this

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:32.640
was a pleasant
surprise with Quarto

00:37:32.640 --> 00:37:37.290
has been that this leveling
up has been a lot smoother.

00:37:37.290 --> 00:37:40.650
The learning curve for
going from a single document

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:43.750
to a multi document project
has been a lot smoother.

00:37:43.750 --> 00:37:46.000
So I'll tell the story
of one of my students.

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:47.200
So this is Holland Stam.

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:49.560
She's an art history
and visual media

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:53.100
studies student who I met when
she was taking my regression

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:53.710
course

00:37:53.710 --> 00:37:55.030
just last spring.

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:58.590
Holland came to my class with
just one semester of intro data

00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:02.710
science under her belt and where
she had learned R Markdown.

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:04.710
And in my class we
drop these students

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:09.060
into learning Quarto after
a brief overview of how

00:38:09.060 --> 00:38:10.800
it's different than R Markdown.

00:38:10.800 --> 00:38:13.920
And so she was doing her
homework assignments and stuff

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:15.660
as single documents
in R Markdown,

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.930
but at the same time working
on her art history thesis, which

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:20.410
was pretty quantitative.

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:22.980
So if you have ever
taken an art history class,

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:27.120
you might be familiar
with these two textbooks.

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:32.170
Gardner's 'Art Through the
Ages' and Janson's history of Art

00:38:32.170 --> 00:38:34.800
These are foundational
Art history textbooks

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:36.900
that have been
around for decades

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:39.220
at this point with
multiple editions.

00:38:39.220 --> 00:38:43.110
And what Holland was looking
for is representation

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:45.960
of different genders and
ethnicities of artists

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:49.170
and how much space is given
to them in these textbooks

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:51.850
and how this changes, if at

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:54.130
All of this has changed
throughout the editions.

00:38:54.130 --> 00:38:56.920
You can see her meticulously
measuring in this picture.

00:38:56.920 --> 00:38:59.670
This is exactly what she
did to collect her data,

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:03.600
actually measure the art that
is featured in the textbooks

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:06.600
through the editions, and
then join this information

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:08.940
with demographic
data on the artists

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:11.820
so that we could analyze and
kind of try to build a model

00:39:11.820 --> 00:39:14.370
to see if things have
changed over the years, what

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:16.890
representation looks like.

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:18.660
On the sad side,
the representation

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:21.670
was exactly what you would
imagine, the lack thereof.

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:24.420
But on the happier
side of this story,

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:26.100
as she was working
on her thesis,

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:29.940
she realized each of my
chapters could be just

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.780
like one of my homework files.

00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:34.180
Because ultimately, what
do we do in our homework?

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:37.180
Write a bit of code, get some
results out, interpret it.

00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:39.390
And that's exactly
what she was doing.

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:41.580
And then with a bit
of Googling around

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:43.710
and a little bit
of help for me, she

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:45.900
was able to bring
these chapters together

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:47.830
with a single YAML file.

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:52.140
And turned it into her thesis.

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:55.020
Now, this wasn't
without friction, right?

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:58.780
This sort of leveling
up for is not easy.

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:02.700
However, when she realized
that she can add captions

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:06.510
to her figures as she creates
them in the code chunks

00:40:06.510 --> 00:40:08.850
and that she can,
she does not have

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:14.100
to manually style citations,
millions of citations

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:17.070
she had for this thesis
project that she can just

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:19.960
use a visual editor to
insert these citations.

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:24.900
She decided I am willing to
put in the effort to do that.

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:29.580
So as a side story, Holland was
in my kind of research lab,

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:34.170
and she also worked with another
student of mine, Sara Lemus,

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:36.130
and they packaged up this data.

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:39.900
So Sara was incredibly
inspired by Holland's enthusiasm

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:41.080
about this project.

00:40:41.080 --> 00:40:43.170
And Sara is a stat
student who had

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:45.600
been working with me
over that semester

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:48.600
to learn a little bit
of package development

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:51.550
by updating some of the
data packages I maintained.

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:54.540
So it was very clear to
us that the enthusiasm

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:57.780
between these two students, they
should come together and work

00:40:57.780 --> 00:40:59.080
on this project together.

00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:00.810
So if you're
interested in the data

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:04.170
that Holland collected and Sara
and Holland put together,

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:06.220
you can actually get
this package off CRAN.

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:11.370
Now, it's called arthistory
and has a really cute hex.

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:12.100
All right.

00:41:12.100 --> 00:41:14.950
So just like Holland.

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:18.700
Quarto has solved a lot of
my problems as an instructor

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:22.820
as well, so I am going to
demo a few of those to you.

00:41:22.820 --> 00:41:26.620
Now, first and foremost, you
can open a Quarto document

00:41:26.620 --> 00:41:28.900
in the RStudio IDE and render it.

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:30.290
Now, I've shown you this before.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:31.820
Why am I saying it again?

00:41:31.820 --> 00:41:33.470
I do not have to
install anything.

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:34.850
Everything comes bundled.

00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:37.180
So if you are
teaching in the RStudio IDE,

00:41:37.180 --> 00:41:41.060
no installation required and
we can basically get going.

00:41:41.060 --> 00:41:44.320
And I think to me, that's
incredibly liberating.

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:47.600
Beyond that, though, there's
really handy features.

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:52.030
For example, with a single YAML
field code-link set to true

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:54.220
I can each of the
documents that I

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:57.890
create can now look a little
bit like package documentation.

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:00.520
You may have seen where
the functions are clickable

00:42:00.520 --> 00:42:04.360
and it takes you directly
to the documentation

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:05.600
for that function.

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:10.120
And that is incredibly helpful
for learning and teaching

00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:14.200
because it saves me a lot of
time from adding those links.

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:17.500
Now on the learning side,
let's try to make a mistake

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:19.910
and let's see that
it's really hard.

00:42:19.910 --> 00:42:22.640
I want to write FALSE
in capital letters.

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:24.050
I'm trying to make a mistake.

00:42:24.050 --> 00:42:26.620
Quarto is constantly telling
me, don't do it, Mine,

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:29.140
but let's go ahead and
think that I actually

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:30.680
managed to do that.

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:33.260
And let's render this document.

00:42:33.260 --> 00:42:36.730
The error that we
get is human readable

00:42:36.730 --> 00:42:39.200
and it tells me
exactly what to do.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:43.550
It says maybe you should try
false in small letters instead.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:47.560
And this is incredibly helpful
for new learners and also

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:50.080
experts who might be
frustrated reading

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:52.570
through cryptic YAML
errors and really

00:42:52.570 --> 00:42:56.150
kind of pushes you in the right
direction to get things going.

00:42:56.150 --> 00:42:58.960
So that again, you can
focus on your content

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:01.600
and not the minutia in a way.

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:05.440
Now, as I said, as an
educator, a lot of slide decks

00:43:05.440 --> 00:43:06.800
is kind of like my life.

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:10.180
So let's turn this into
revealjs presentation

00:43:10.180 --> 00:43:12.820
again and let me show you
a few of the handy features

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:15.130
here that I use regularly.

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:18.190
The fact that you get a
document outline by default

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:19.040
is really nice.

00:43:19.040 --> 00:43:22.840
If somebody loses their
place, everyone always asks,

00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:24.740
can I get a PDF of the slides?

00:43:24.740 --> 00:43:29.290
Now they can without you
having to do anything extra.

00:43:29.290 --> 00:43:31.240
Other things that
are really handy

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:34.420
is being able to annotate
your slides so we

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:36.580
can turn on the
chalkboard feature,

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:39.910
and this way you can go
to something like a plot

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:42.550
and start outlining things.

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:45.370
If you like me have been an
educator through the pandemic,

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:47.570
I bet you spent hours
in faculty meetings.

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:49.940
How are we going to show
things to students on Zoom?

00:43:49.940 --> 00:43:52.610
Well, this is one way
that we can do this.

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:57.010
We can also open a
chalkboard and just

00:43:57.010 --> 00:43:59.680
start writing on it, which
is really handy when you're

00:43:59.680 --> 00:44:02.200
teaching in a classroom where
there's a projector screen

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:03.880
but nothing else.

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:05.860
I can also turn on
multiplex, which

00:44:05.860 --> 00:44:08.860
is the feature we're using right
now if you're following along

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:12.620
and it will move forward
your slides for you.

00:44:12.620 --> 00:44:14.320
So if your students
fall asleep, they

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:18.280
can sort of stay with you
as you go through things.

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:21.670
In order to do this, you open
the speaker file and kind

00:44:21.670 --> 00:44:26.770
of present from that and post
the nonspeaker file for them.

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:31.510
Another thing I'm going to do
here is to turn on the code

00:44:31.510 --> 00:44:34.120
so that it is visible,
because when I'm teaching,

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:38.380
I would like to show the code to
my students and let's show one

00:44:38.380 --> 00:44:39.710
more handy feature.

00:44:39.710 --> 00:44:42.160
Oftentimes when you have
a big chunk of code,

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:44.950
like a lengthy
ggplot code, you may

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:47.650
want to iterate through that
highlighting things line

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:48.410
by line.

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:50.710
So with one chunk
option, you can

00:44:50.710 --> 00:44:52.990
define which lines
you want to highlight,

00:44:52.990 --> 00:44:55.780
and it automatically
adds animation to your

00:44:55.780 --> 00:44:58.490
slides so that you
can step through them.

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:00.490
And to me, that's
really helpful because I

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:04.150
didn't have to add characters
to my code to do that.

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:05.840
Now my plot is tiny.

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:08.620
That's not helpful.

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:12.520
But again, without me having
to do extra work to move it

00:45:12.520 --> 00:45:15.550
on the next slide, I can say
change the output location

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:18.700
to next slide and it will
automatically do that for me

00:45:18.700 --> 00:45:23.440
without me referring back to an
older chunk label or something

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:24.050
like that.

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:26.860
So again, a lot of these
things, many of you

00:45:26.860 --> 00:45:29.530
might know how to solve,
but I think the fact

00:45:29.530 --> 00:45:34.090
that they're solved so
elegantly is quite liberating.

00:45:34.090 --> 00:45:35.680
The last thing I'm
going to talk about

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:38.390
is what about the
research side of my life?

00:45:38.390 --> 00:45:42.730
Well, let's open a new
project and let's go ahead

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:44.290
and write a paper.

00:45:44.290 --> 00:45:47.110
I'm going to submit a paper
to the Journal of American

00:45:47.110 --> 00:45:51.680
Statistical Association
with one click.

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:54.340
I can basically
from the terminal

00:45:54.340 --> 00:45:56.740
use one of the templates
that are available

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:59.480
and there's a growing
number of templates there.

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:01.012
Should I trust the authors?

00:46:01.012 --> 00:46:01.970
What do you guys think?

00:46:01.970 --> 00:46:04.270
Should we trust the
Quarto development team?

00:46:04.270 --> 00:46:06.260
I think I'm going
to say, yeah, OK.

00:46:06.260 --> 00:46:10.360
And I'm going to call
this 'my awesome paper'.

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:13.090
This will generate
a template for me

00:46:13.090 --> 00:46:14.930
that I can author in Quarto.

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:18.340
Which means I can put
my code and my text

00:46:18.340 --> 00:46:20.950
in the same document,
which is how I generally

00:46:20.950 --> 00:46:22.850
like to write my code.

00:46:22.850 --> 00:46:25.438
And another nice thing
here is that if I

00:46:25.438 --> 00:46:26.980
was to get rejected
from this journal

00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:30.340
and wanted to submit to another
one, changing between styles

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:35.080
is pretty easy again without
changing your source code.

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:38.390
So let's go ahead
and give it a title.

00:46:38.390 --> 00:46:40.690
My awesome paper that's
ready to be submitted

00:46:40.690 --> 00:46:43.030
and I'll put my
name as first author

00:46:43.030 --> 00:46:45.110
and we can render
it to a document.

00:46:45.110 --> 00:46:46.600
And if any of you
in the audience

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:49.030
have used the
ASA style before,

00:46:49.030 --> 00:46:51.990
the output should look
pretty familiar to you.

00:46:56.310 --> 00:46:56.850
Woo! woo! [applause]

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:05.820
Now there's unfortunately
one drawback.

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:09.690
Quarto does not write
your paper for you.

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:12.090
But my experience with the
Quarto development team

00:47:12.090 --> 00:47:13.680
is that when you
open issues, they're

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:15.400
really quick to respond.

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:16.890
So I don't know,
maybe by the end

00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:18.307
of the week they'll
figure out how

00:47:18.307 --> 00:47:21.380
to write our papers for us to.

00:47:21.380 --> 00:47:22.080
All right.

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:26.120
So we've talked about
flattening the learning curve

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:30.920
and, you know, basically saying
that when we are leveling up

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:34.820
from a single document
to a multi document input

00:47:34.820 --> 00:47:39.170
and going to various
output types,

00:47:39.170 --> 00:47:42.780
and I said that this was
a pleasant surprise to me.

00:47:42.780 --> 00:47:45.560
In hindsight, it should
not have been a surprise.

00:47:45.560 --> 00:47:47.840
It's just not what I was
thinking when I first

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:49.580
started teaching with Quarto.

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:52.280
But if you think about
the features of Quarto

00:47:52.280 --> 00:47:57.200
like consistent citation
syntax across various formats,

00:47:57.200 --> 00:48:01.460
more intentional control over
when you should run and rerun

00:48:01.460 --> 00:48:07.640
your computation, and a single,
minimal human readable YAML

00:48:07.640 --> 00:48:12.740
that controls inputs, layouts
and theming everything in one

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:14.120
file.

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:17.690
All of these features contribute
to flattening that curve

00:48:17.690 --> 00:48:23.360
for creating more
complex outputs.

00:48:23.360 --> 00:48:27.470
But beyond just
growing and expanding

00:48:27.470 --> 00:48:30.650
the scope of your
work with Quarto,

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:34.070
I think that Quarto can grow
with learners in a profound way

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:35.090
as well.

00:48:35.090 --> 00:48:39.530
The fact that Quarto is
language and independent

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:42.140
and also authoring
tool independent

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:45.440
offers an opportunity for
folks to take Quarto with them

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:47.780
in their data science
journey, wherever

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:49.500
that journey might take them.

00:48:49.500 --> 00:48:54.530
I mean, after working on this
for a year almost with Julia,

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:59.780
I am almost tempted to learn
Python and maybe I will.

00:48:59.780 --> 00:49:00.900
But you know what?

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:01.980
Who is learning

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:04.370
Python are my
students, and they're

00:49:04.370 --> 00:49:07.670
learning Python and Julia
and other languages,

00:49:07.670 --> 00:49:10.520
mostly in their computer
science courses.

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:15.980
And to imagine that they could
learn R in RStudio with Quarto

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:19.430
in my class and then go take
a computer science class

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:22.220
where they're probably
living in a Jupyter Notebook

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:25.410
and learning Python
but still using Quarto.

00:49:25.410 --> 00:49:29.630
That sounds like an incredibly
bright future to me.

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:31.700
And a bright future
includes things

00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:33.950
we can't imagine yet as well.

00:49:33.950 --> 00:49:38.090
So Julia will now tell us a bit
about reimagining with Quarto.

00:49:38.090 --> 00:49:40.590
Thanks, Mine.

00:49:40.590 --> 00:49:42.810
So we've already
seen how much Quarto

00:49:42.810 --> 00:49:47.970
can do for teaching and
collaborating and sharing.

00:49:47.970 --> 00:49:50.790
And now I want to
push it a bit further.

00:49:50.790 --> 00:49:54.120
What if we could reimagine open
science as part of the climate

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:55.260
movement?

00:49:55.260 --> 00:49:58.080
We know what this R community
and the open science

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:01.830
and open source
communities are capable of.

00:50:01.830 --> 00:50:06.250
So what if we put that
energy towards climate?

00:50:06.250 --> 00:50:08.260
Because we're in a
climate emergency,

00:50:08.260 --> 00:50:12.820
we must rapidly and
radically reshape society.

00:50:12.820 --> 00:50:16.510
We need every solution
and every solver.

00:50:16.510 --> 00:50:19.000
This is hard and it's big.

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:23.320
And my hope lies is in
this community and the climate

00:50:23.320 --> 00:50:25.990
researchers and everyone
in this movement

00:50:25.990 --> 00:50:30.600
who feels like what they do
every single day plays a part.

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:34.350
So let's think about this
concretely with three user

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:38.190
groups in this community
of in this movement.

00:50:38.190 --> 00:50:42.360
First, our scientists
who are working directly

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:44.290
in line with climate change.

00:50:44.290 --> 00:50:45.960
This could be
fisheries scientists

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:50.610
who are studying where our
food sources are migrating

00:50:50.610 --> 00:50:53.910
to as the oceans warm and
operating with managers

00:50:53.910 --> 00:50:56.670
and stakeholders there.

00:50:56.670 --> 00:51:00.090
They're working with really hard
problems, these really hard,

00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:00.970
big problems.

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:03.570
And they can also feel
stuck on the little problems

00:51:03.570 --> 00:51:08.010
of moving files around and
interoperating with each other.

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:10.590
That's really where
openscapes has been,

00:51:10.590 --> 00:51:13.800
really bringing this
passion and this movement

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:17.280
that we feel in the community
to help these researchers

00:51:17.280 --> 00:51:20.250
and help connect them there.

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:23.190
And I think now so many
of you that I talked to

00:51:23.190 --> 00:51:26.880
in the open source and
R communities in this,

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:32.490
so the second category is open
science and R communities.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:34.810
A lot of you are
feeling stuck too.

00:51:34.810 --> 00:51:38.040
You're feeling the
pressure on climate

00:51:38.040 --> 00:51:41.610
and feeling like changing to
more efficient light bulbs

00:51:41.610 --> 00:51:42.960
isn't enough.

00:51:42.960 --> 00:51:47.250
What if you aligned your daily
work as part of climate action

00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:48.510
as well?

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:51.730
We want Posit to be
around in 100 years.

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:54.390
What is 100 years going
to look like if we

00:51:54.390 --> 00:51:59.070
don't act now and really
put this effort together?

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:02.310
And the third category in
theme with the conference

00:52:02.310 --> 00:52:07.420
is extendable
beyond these two categories.

00:52:07.420 --> 00:52:10.210
So we really need every
solution and every solver

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:16.040
across all facets of geographies
and people and skill level.

00:52:16.040 --> 00:52:19.700
And experience with technology.

00:52:19.700 --> 00:52:25.040
So this work is really hard and
we'll return to concrete things

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:26.670
that we can do in a moment.

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:29.570
But this really does
mean slowing down a bit

00:52:29.570 --> 00:52:34.880
in order to speed up, but
it speeds up like a flywheel.

00:52:34.880 --> 00:52:38.360
My colleague Ileana Fenwick
is a marine ecologist

00:52:38.360 --> 00:52:41.780
who participated in open
scapes earlier last year

00:52:41.780 --> 00:52:46.430
and she really quickly
became a member

00:52:46.430 --> 00:52:49.310
of the team teaching and
extending and connecting

00:52:49.310 --> 00:52:52.340
other communities and
seeing what was possible.

00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:56.080
And the way she describes
this is beautiful.

00:52:56.080 --> 00:52:58.450
Because of Openscapes,
I've renewed hope

00:52:58.450 --> 00:53:01.800
In how we can bring about
a kinder future in science.

00:53:01.800 --> 00:53:04.330
I see a commitment in the
open science community

00:53:04.330 --> 00:53:07.450
to bridging the gaps where
we've left members of our

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:09.140
own community behind,

00:53:09.140 --> 00:53:12.920
And it inspires me.

00:53:12.920 --> 00:53:16.280
So we're talking about a
profound culture shift that

00:53:16.280 --> 00:53:18.680
involves real,
deliberate work on both

00:53:18.680 --> 00:53:20.840
the tech and the human side.

00:53:20.840 --> 00:53:23.480
And we've got the tech
side really figured out

00:53:23.480 --> 00:53:26.510
it's where the momentum
and the potential

00:53:26.510 --> 00:53:29.540
and what we're all doing
is amazing and phenomenal.

00:53:29.540 --> 00:53:33.410
And we can bring that power and
energy to the human side, to we

00:53:33.410 --> 00:53:36.440
can build inclusion
into our daily work.

00:53:36.440 --> 00:53:39.770
So let's think about the teams
or the colleagues or the people

00:53:39.770 --> 00:53:43.190
that you interact with, because
I think that they are probably

00:53:43.190 --> 00:53:47.840
way more similar to the groups I
work with than we might expect.

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:50.720
First of all, it means
talking to colleagues,

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:52.570
asking them how they're doing.

00:53:52.570 --> 00:53:53.890
Are they stuck?

00:53:53.890 --> 00:53:56.100
What is their
workflow look like?

00:53:56.100 --> 00:53:58.020
Where is their friction?

00:53:58.020 --> 00:54:00.700
Who's contributing
to our workflow?

00:54:00.700 --> 00:54:01.890
Where are the handoffs?

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:04.080
Maybe between reports or data?

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:06.260
Where are people
feeling friction?

00:54:06.260 --> 00:54:08.540
What can we do to help?

00:54:08.540 --> 00:54:10.670
So this means asking
all these questions,

00:54:10.670 --> 00:54:12.980
but really, and
that's hard enough,

00:54:12.980 --> 00:54:16.520
those open-ended questions.

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:18.920
Another hard part is listening.

00:54:18.920 --> 00:54:21.470
Listening is really hard.

00:54:21.470 --> 00:54:25.130
It takes it means that
you are not trying

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:26.930
to assume what they need.

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:28.820
You're not trying
to solutioneer

00:54:28.820 --> 00:54:32.070
as soon as you hear
something, because oftentimes,

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:35.070
we don't even have language
to describe where we're stuck.

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:38.110
And that's part of the problem.

00:54:38.110 --> 00:54:40.690
So it also means
then stepping up

00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:44.360
and being braver to ask for
help and say what you need.

00:54:44.360 --> 00:54:47.170
And really create
this culture where

00:54:47.170 --> 00:54:49.930
what we feel in the R
community around R code

00:54:49.930 --> 00:54:52.810
is also applicable to climate.

00:54:52.810 --> 00:54:57.010
So this is a practice to
develop daily, to work on,

00:54:57.010 --> 00:54:58.280
to help others with.

00:54:58.280 --> 00:54:59.450
It's not a checklist.

00:54:59.450 --> 00:55:02.290
It's a daily thing
that we can all do.

00:55:02.290 --> 00:55:06.980
So here is a practical
example of this.

00:55:06.980 --> 00:55:10.190
Gavin Fay's lab group
at UMass Dartmouth

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:14.940
created the Fay lab manual
together during the pandemic.

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:17.490
Here they so they are
quantitative fisheries

00:55:17.490 --> 00:55:18.490
ecologists.

00:55:18.490 --> 00:55:23.400
They work with data and code
to work on fisheries, things

00:55:23.400 --> 00:55:26.670
that I just described
a bit earlier.

00:55:26.670 --> 00:55:32.850
So it is uncommon for people
in the environmental field

00:55:32.850 --> 00:55:35.980
to have data science training
in the universities right now.

00:55:35.980 --> 00:55:38.460
So that means people coming
into this quantitative group

00:55:38.460 --> 00:55:42.310
have varying levels of
expertise and experience.

00:55:42.310 --> 00:55:44.830
And so they wrote this
lab manual as a way

00:55:44.830 --> 00:55:47.470
to not only write down
their lab philosophy,

00:55:47.470 --> 00:55:50.290
their code of conduct, how
they wanted to work together.

00:55:50.290 --> 00:55:53.260
They talked about
where, how to name files

00:55:53.260 --> 00:55:55.390
and also how to learn
the tools that they're

00:55:55.390 --> 00:55:57.220
going to develop together.

00:55:57.220 --> 00:56:03.230
So this is a living online open
document, and it's imperfect.

00:56:03.230 --> 00:56:06.610
And it's radical in
science, where we often

00:56:06.610 --> 00:56:10.670
wait until after the research
is done to publish something.

00:56:10.670 --> 00:56:13.030
And that happens
years into a career

00:56:13.030 --> 00:56:15.800
and really limits
the participation.

00:56:15.800 --> 00:56:18.210
Folks feel they have
an agency they have.

00:56:21.220 --> 00:56:23.963
So this is radical in
three ways or in many ways.

00:56:23.963 --> 00:56:25.380
But let me tell
you three of them.

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:28.450
One is that it makes
the implicit explicit.

00:56:28.450 --> 00:56:31.970
The team all know because
they co-created the document.

00:56:31.970 --> 00:56:35.500
They're all on the same
page, literally Quarto page.

00:56:35.500 --> 00:56:37.570
And to understand how
they work together,

00:56:37.570 --> 00:56:40.655
they're able to put their values
forward and not only reduce

00:56:40.655 --> 00:56:43.030
the amount of emails where
people are asking where things

00:56:43.030 --> 00:56:45.790
are, but also really
put this out there

00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:47.960
and welcome other
people to join them.

00:56:47.960 --> 00:56:51.610
And when folks are interested
in joining this lab group,

00:56:51.610 --> 00:56:56.020
they mention this document
as a welcoming reason.

00:56:56.020 --> 00:56:59.290
A second thing is that
this document onboards

00:56:59.290 --> 00:57:01.760
learners as contributors.

00:57:01.760 --> 00:57:04.210
So first of all, there
is an onboarding process

00:57:04.210 --> 00:57:06.940
and it's written in the site.

00:57:06.940 --> 00:57:10.510
And so it instructs you not only
to how the lab works, but also

00:57:10.510 --> 00:57:14.810
how you can use Quarto to fix a
typo to update a link you saw.

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:17.030
So you're contributing
to the lab group.

00:57:17.030 --> 00:57:20.110
You're, you're
joining in that way

00:57:20.110 --> 00:57:24.170
and you're contributing
to science on day one.

00:57:24.170 --> 00:57:28.060
And that is just amazing to
have that kind of empowerment

00:57:28.060 --> 00:57:30.310
and imagine where what
you feel like you're

00:57:30.310 --> 00:57:32.470
going to do in science next.

00:57:32.470 --> 00:57:35.880
So this is hugely
radical for science.

00:57:35.880 --> 00:57:39.660
And the third most radical
thing is the investment

00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:43.290
they put into psychological
safety and growth mindsets

00:57:43.290 --> 00:57:44.710
in their group.

00:57:44.710 --> 00:57:47.650
Psychological safety
is the idea that it's

00:57:47.650 --> 00:57:49.540
a shared understanding
within a team,

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:53.470
that team is safe for
interpersonal risk taking.

00:57:53.470 --> 00:57:56.920
That means that people feel
comfortable to ask a question

00:57:56.920 --> 00:57:59.380
if they don't something
that they feel like they

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:03.010
can give and receive feedback.

00:58:03.010 --> 00:58:05.920
So this is really important,
along with a growth mindset

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:09.270
where everyone can
learn from everyone.

00:58:09.270 --> 00:58:13.660
This is the power of yet:
I don't know this yet.

00:58:13.660 --> 00:58:17.060
I can't do
this yet, but I can.

00:58:17.060 --> 00:58:19.030
And that kind of
mindset and learning

00:58:19.030 --> 00:58:25.480
across the hierarchy of science,
across expertise and skills,

00:58:25.480 --> 00:58:27.640
thinking that we all have
something to contribute

00:58:27.640 --> 00:58:29.800
is really important.

00:58:29.800 --> 00:58:33.040
So reimagining within
this one lab group then

00:58:33.040 --> 00:58:35.590
can scale that this
slowing down can then

00:58:35.590 --> 00:58:39.100
have rapid effects with that
speeding up and amplifying.

00:58:39.100 --> 00:58:43.660
So really quickly after this,
we forked the Fay labs idea

00:58:43.660 --> 00:58:48.310
and wrote our own Quarto
approach guide for Openscapes.

00:58:48.310 --> 00:58:51.880
This is how we work together and
onboard ourselves and onboard

00:58:51.880 --> 00:58:53.980
others to the team
and also other groups

00:58:53.980 --> 00:58:57.340
are able to use it to. The
California Water Boards are

00:58:57.340 --> 00:59:01.660
leading their first Openscapes
sessions this summer

00:59:01.660 --> 00:59:05.350
and at NOAA fisheries they
saw what NASA was doing

00:59:05.350 --> 00:59:07.640
and forked that
idea with Quarto.

00:59:07.640 --> 00:59:10.750
So they are using
Quarto for a place

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:14.800
to build community across their
NOAA science centers for Open

00:59:14.800 --> 00:59:17.740
Science and to connect
and to empower and enable

00:59:17.740 --> 00:59:21.630
these early adopters
within each of these silos.

00:59:21.630 --> 00:59:25.860
So these are examples of
Quarto for culture change

00:59:25.860 --> 00:59:27.010
and it's amazing.

00:59:27.010 --> 00:59:29.220
We can do this with
the same tooling

00:59:29.220 --> 00:59:31.380
that we're using for our
reproducible research

00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:32.670
at the same time.

00:59:32.670 --> 00:59:35.550
These documents from
where they are now

00:59:35.550 --> 00:59:37.420
don't have any code in them.

00:59:37.420 --> 00:59:38.530
They're there.

00:59:38.530 --> 00:59:40.830
And Quarto is a
really friendly place

00:59:40.830 --> 00:59:43.710
for folks to contribute no
matter where their comfort

00:59:43.710 --> 00:59:45.540
level is with code.

00:59:45.540 --> 00:59:46.720
And that's really important.

00:59:46.720 --> 00:59:49.080
We've already seen
features of Quarto

00:59:49.080 --> 00:59:50.470
that are really friendly.

00:59:50.470 --> 00:59:55.290
And with guardrails to help
new learners contribute.

00:59:55.290 --> 00:59:58.290
And another way for this,
for newcomers to contribute

00:59:58.290 --> 01:00:01.380
is in the visual
editor and I'm

01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:04.660
going to do one final demo
here about the visual editor.

01:00:04.660 --> 01:00:08.280
But I also want to emphasize
that while I'm well,

01:00:08.280 --> 01:00:11.110
we're going to look at it
through the lens of a new user.

01:00:11.110 --> 01:00:14.740
This is a really powerful
tool for experienced users.

01:00:14.740 --> 01:00:18.780
And so as part of this
mindset of helping

01:00:18.780 --> 01:00:21.510
welcome other people,
as I walk through this,

01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:23.880
maybe consider how you
might use this approach

01:00:23.880 --> 01:00:25.560
to welcome one of
your colleagues

01:00:25.560 --> 01:00:30.020
who might be less, less sure
about this kind of tech.

01:00:37.840 --> 01:00:42.520
So here we are in the IDE and
I've opened Mine's home page,

01:00:42.520 --> 01:00:45.310
her home page Quarto
document and I'm immediately

01:00:45.310 --> 01:00:47.955
going to go to the visual
editor like she showed before.

01:00:50.750 --> 01:00:55.430
So I can type in Quarto
in the visual editor

01:00:55.430 --> 01:00:59.450
and then I can use the menu bars
up here as I would in a Google

01:00:59.450 --> 01:01:02.270
Doc to make a header.

01:01:02.270 --> 01:01:06.620
And as I type I can
also highlight words

01:01:06.620 --> 01:01:08.250
and make them italic.

01:01:08.250 --> 01:01:10.760
I can use keyboard
shortcuts like command

01:01:10.760 --> 01:01:12.293
I to turn off the italics.

01:01:15.440 --> 01:01:19.680
And this is a way that I can
comfortably write my text.

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:22.060
I can also paste formatted text.

01:01:22.060 --> 01:01:23.520
This is a way we use.

01:01:23.520 --> 01:01:26.210
Quarto quite often is
by interoperating

01:01:26.210 --> 01:01:28.070
between other documents
that we're using,

01:01:28.070 --> 01:01:31.340
and it's amazing that it
saves that formatting.

01:01:31.340 --> 01:01:34.290
If we want to add an image.

01:01:34.290 --> 01:01:37.050
We can use the
Insert feature here

01:01:37.050 --> 01:01:38.760
and see all of these
different things

01:01:38.760 --> 01:01:40.640
that we'd be able to insert.

01:01:40.640 --> 01:01:43.470
Right now we'll insert
an image and we could

01:01:43.470 --> 01:01:45.965
type this or browse a file.

01:01:49.290 --> 01:01:52.060
I'll speed this up for
the purpose of our demo.

01:01:52.060 --> 01:01:54.030
In a moment we can
set center, but I'm

01:01:54.030 --> 01:01:58.210
going to paste the caption and
the Alt text for us right now.

01:01:58.210 --> 01:02:00.150
And then we can press OK.

01:02:00.150 --> 01:02:02.500
And this is now in
the visual editor.

01:02:02.500 --> 01:02:05.100
We can see it, we can
understand how it interoperates

01:02:05.100 --> 01:02:08.580
with our document and we
can also resize it there

01:02:08.580 --> 01:02:12.300
on the fly, which is awesome.

01:02:12.300 --> 01:02:14.040
If we're interested
in the source code,

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:15.140
let's have a look at it.

01:02:17.880 --> 01:02:21.960
And you can see down here on
line 15 is where the image is

01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:24.090
and that where the
cursor is, we've

01:02:24.090 --> 01:02:26.480
got the width and the
figure line center.

01:02:26.480 --> 01:02:28.530
That's what we had
done with our mouse,

01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:31.950
but that's now in
Quarto for us.

01:02:31.950 --> 01:02:34.930
So back in the visual editor.

01:02:34.930 --> 01:02:38.170
Let's save this and click
that render on Save button

01:02:38.170 --> 01:02:42.820
that will then let us see
this live rendered output

01:02:42.820 --> 01:02:45.050
alongside the visual editor.

01:02:45.050 --> 01:02:47.420
So I love that render
on Save button.

01:02:47.420 --> 01:02:49.660
When I don't have a lot
of heavy computation

01:02:49.660 --> 01:02:51.730
so that I can every
time I save, I

01:02:51.730 --> 01:02:53.300
get to see exactly how it looks.

01:02:53.300 --> 01:02:55.420
Beautiful over here.

01:02:55.420 --> 01:03:00.460
OK we can fix this typo with
those nice red squiggles lines

01:03:00.460 --> 01:03:01.820
and let's add a citation.

01:03:01.820 --> 01:03:03.830
Let's add a citation
for the tidyverse.

01:03:03.830 --> 01:03:08.170
We can do this with a
command a or sorry, @ symbol.

01:03:08.170 --> 01:03:10.490
And pull up my Zotero library.

01:03:10.490 --> 01:03:12.680
That's all that it's
found on my computer.

01:03:12.680 --> 01:03:14.110
I can type.

01:03:14.110 --> 01:03:15.180
Whew sorry.

01:03:15.180 --> 01:03:15.680
I've got.

01:03:18.410 --> 01:03:22.770
Well, I can type either
Wickham or tidy or I'm sure

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:24.330
many other things.

01:03:24.330 --> 01:03:29.160
When I add when I save
again, it's there already

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:33.100
in the document and I've got
the references down here.

01:03:33.100 --> 01:03:33.800
This is amazing.

01:03:40.420 --> 01:03:45.553
A second way you can do it is
to use the forward slash and click citation.

01:03:48.490 --> 01:03:53.600
And then you're able to actually
even paste a DOI on the fly,

01:03:53.600 --> 01:03:56.770
and it will find it for you.

01:03:56.770 --> 01:04:06.440
Yes! You can see
when I save this,

01:04:06.440 --> 01:04:09.133
it's in a different
style, which is great

01:04:09.133 --> 01:04:10.550
that you have that
kind of control

01:04:10.550 --> 01:04:11.860
right here from the browser.

01:04:14.967 --> 01:04:16.050
Or from the visual editor.

01:04:16.050 --> 01:04:16.590
I'm sorry.

01:04:21.390 --> 01:04:23.280
I have a few more
features to show here,

01:04:23.280 --> 01:04:25.500
but I'm thinking in
the interest of time,

01:04:25.500 --> 01:04:30.100
we'll let you watch this
online by yourself later.

01:04:30.100 --> 01:04:34.000
So I want to bring this
back as we close our keynote

01:04:34.000 --> 01:04:36.790
and just say again that
we are all part of this.

01:04:36.790 --> 01:04:39.850
We've been talking
about what Quarto enables

01:04:39.850 --> 01:04:41.260
throughout this
talk, but this is

01:04:41.260 --> 01:04:44.330
about what we are able to
reimagine at all levels.

01:04:44.330 --> 01:04:47.530
We can all play our part to
do this in our daily work

01:04:47.530 --> 01:04:50.090
and with our teams
and scale and beyond.

01:04:50.090 --> 01:04:54.280
So we invite you to reimagine
with us because we cannot we

01:04:54.280 --> 01:04:57.590
must not go it alone.

01:04:57.590 --> 01:05:02.180
So if you would like to pick
up your Quarto journey to share,

01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:05.000
collaborate, teach or
reimagine where we left off,

01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:07.610
go to this link for
our slides and you're

01:05:07.610 --> 01:05:10.760
going to see that we have
a few more slides for you

01:05:10.760 --> 01:05:12.390
with resources to learn.

01:05:12.390 --> 01:05:16.430
Quarto and also
references to some of the

01:05:16.430 --> 01:05:17.940
works that we've cited.

01:05:17.940 --> 01:05:19.730
And there's a
Quarto session later

